<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · infrastructure</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanzania–Zambia Railway</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1970">1970</a>–<a href="/years/1975">1975</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as TAZARA, TAZARA Railway, Uhuru Railway</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tanzania–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zambia">Zambia</a> Railway is a railway of about 1,860 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a> to Kapiri Mposhi in Zambia, built between 1970 and 1975 with an interest-free loan, engineers and workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. It gave landlocked Zambia an outlet to the sea that avoided white-ruled Rhodesia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, and was then the largest Chinese aid project abroad.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanzania-zambia-railway.jpg" alt="Tanzania–Zambia Railway" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jkan997 · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Railways_in_Tanzania.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
